up from clinton  will actually populate  the civic database with their findings  and so i think that this is a really  cool  collaboration that that will make sure  that a lot of the prominence and that  experience  are in in civic  also they've had a recent integration  with protein paint  which is part of the st juice pecan data  portal that i'll talk about on the next  slide  so i want to show a couple of  screenshots from civic  i find the browse function to be really  helpful  in civic and so when you go into the  browse function you can type  into any of the search bars  towards the top of the table here i've  typed in coenzio carcinoma  and what populates below are all of the  variants  that are associated with  polentiocarcinoma  in acidic so this is a really helpful  way to  again kind of do that cross-checking um  qc review  of variants in a specific cancer type  and there are several other ways to use  this as well  and um also in civic one of the really  cool  things about civic is how they display  evidence and so this is one of the  evidence tables  displayed for an egfr amplification  and so in the table you'll see four rows  and these  represent four different pieces of  primary literature  and the icons in the table are coded for  for recognition so you can see  um this first line actually means that  it's a course of poor  prognosis and then the next three lines  you'll see  uh drugs listed in the drug column and  the table and these are  related to more predictive or  therapeutic implications  um but when you actually click on one of  the rows or one of the pieces of  evidence the panel below this  populates where you can see a one to two  sentence summary  of this piece of evidence and link out  to pubmed to  make your own assertions which i always  recommend  the pediatric cancer data portal is  developed from  saint jude's children's research  hospital  and um i think that they're a real  leader in  visualizations and just visualizing  large sets of data in general um  in addition to the visuals we'll go over  today  i wanted to make aware that this group  also  has some publicly available  bioinformatics workflows including  the pecan pie or the pathogenicity  information exchange  which will classify variants into  different metal levels  so on the right side of this slide at  the top  is a representation of all of the cases  that are  cataloged in the pcan data portal  and they're stratified by disease type  so the yellow is solid tumor  the blue is brain tumor and the red is  hematologic malignancies and then  different subtypes  of within those categories also on the  home page you'll see a word cloud of  different gene names  and you'll see how frequently these  genes are altered  within those disease types kind of  color-coded the same as  the pie chart above  another really powerful visualization  tool in pecan data portal is protein  paint and that's shown below the only  difference between protein paint  and genome paint is within protein paint  you'll only  see the mutations that fall within  exons or coating regions for fusion  [Music]  with genome paint you start to see some  of those more  intron related mutations and you'll  actually look at  a larger transcript of the  gene itself so below is the 801 gene  by um by protein paint  so you can see the lollipop plot there  you also see buttons that connect  um not just the data from keycam data  portal but also  cosmic clinvar and civic which i  referred to  earlier so probably  uh some of my favorite things about this  graph  are that you can see the domains  that are color coded on the protein  itself  and on the bottom rectangle of the  protein and then also if you  click on a fusion  you'll actually see a representation of  the pertinent fusion and understand  which domains from both fusion partners  are  in the fusion transcript in the actual  fusion that was observed  so the jackson clinical knowledge base  and this one actually the other  animation went a little too quick but we  can talk through it  um this is based out of the jackson  laboratory and there's several different  license there are a couple different  licensing tiers i wanted to mention  here there's the ckb core which is the  free license to jack ckb  and this includes kind of the basic um  you know primary driver genes  for cancer and a lot of the therapeutic  information  that it's in jack ckb the boost  really ups the functionality it has a  larger gene list  that the knowledge base covers  and then also adds some of these more  interactive  visual um kind of stunning pieces  within jeff ckb and then the third  license  that we can see right now is a ckg flex  and the ckb flex allows for data  downloads  and kind of more uh interactivity  between  um the ckb and your own lab applications  and then in um in addition  to a lot of more therapeutic  detailed information that you find in  jax ckb  they have this concept of a molecular  profile  that is unique to ckb  and what this means is that you can  actually  look at information  regarding not just one variant but a  combination of variants  and so in one of the panels on this  slide you'll see that we have searched  for an owl rearrangement  and a met amplification and we were able  to  find um information on  this molecular profile and linking to  two pieces of primary literature and  then in the front of the slide it's a  sunburst spot that's available  through ckb boost and this is  annotating or a representation of  all the annotations in jack's cbd  um in relation to the alk genes  so you can further filter your search  through an interactive sunburst plot  which is exciting  the next resource i'll go over is the  molecular oncology almanac  and this is from the broad ending the  farmer or  dana farber sorry and you can search by  biomarker  cancer type level of predictive  multiplication or therapy  and the level of predictive implication  might be my favorite thing about this  because you're so  easily able to identify the pieces of  evidence that make  different alterations a tier one  event so you can you can find your  pieces of evidence that  that make a gene um make a gene and  therapy combination  fda approved like you see on this bottom  panel the top line  you'll see that tsd1 oncogenic mutations  with upper alignment there's  there's a link to the evidence saying  that that is an fda approved therapy  for tsd one oncogenic mutations which is  really really helpful  so the middleman database of chromosome  aberrations  and gene fusions in cancer is funded by  the national cancer institute  and a couple of swedish cancer societies  um it's supported by the institute for  assistance biology cancer genomics  cloud and that's one of the few  cytogenetics  related databases knowledge bases out  there  and so on this database you can search  over  70 000 cases that are curated from the  primary literature  and you can download the entire database  or you can download results from the  search  also i've heard that there's some  visualization coming soon so  stay tuned um probably my favorite  search function within middlemen  is the recurrent chromosome aberration  search  where you can search for a chromosome  ban or a particular  imbalance and it will search the  literature and you'll find  cases with that same alterations  and you can link to the primary  literature you can see the karyotype for  those cases along with  the diagnosis or morphology so it's a  pretty cool  resource  phenomenon mastermind is a literature  search  tool that uses natural language  processing to mine  and prioritize over 30 million articles  and over  two million supplemental data sets there  are two licenses with this product as  well there's a basic license which  allows you to run a simple query  or a professional license which adds a  lot of  functionality and a lot of kind of more  in-depth searches  so you can explore um associations which  i'll go into in the next slide  or a focus view in the pro version so  below  um in the piano below you'll see a  graph with a bunch of circles in it and  each circle represents  a publication and on the y-axis  is the citations per journal and on the  x-axis  is the year and the size of the circle  is corresponds to the impact factor of  the journal  so this is for the x and two genes  this next graph is also on  their user interface and it allows you  to refine your search  to a particular gene domain  or a particular residue within that gene  which is pretty helpful  and i've mentioned that there's a new  association function  in genomic mastermind for the probe  version of mastermind and what you can  do here is really adjust your  search criteria to include  so many things including csvs  therapies diseases variants and then you  can  look at all of your evidence once you've  kind of taken all the pieces that you  want to refine in your search  and i really wanted to touch on a little  bit of data harmonization  by talking about the variant  interpretation for cancer consortium  this group is a driver project  and part of the global alliance for  genomics and health  and what this group did was they took  several different prominent knowledge  bases  and took the elements from these  knowledge bases  and annotated them similarly so you  could  query multiple knowledge bases with one  search and this  is really important the the need for  this  came arose from interrogation  of all the variants within these  knowledge bases and we  found that um  you know most variants were only  annotated  in either one or two knowledge bases and  only eight variants  were annotated across all of these  knowledge bases so that was really  surprising  so when i first gave an iteration of  this talk  back in 2017 um  different uh knowledge bases and  databases were starting  to make connections with each other  through api  or through data sharing or by  referencing  each other in the knowledge bases  themselves  and over the last couple years we've  seen these connections become more  intricate  and become more involved with each other  so we've seen  more integration between different  databases and knowledge bases and  aggregation activity  just the integration so we're not there  yet  but what we're hoping for  is this ideal state where a user  will be able to define it what they want  in a comprehensive resource  and be able to tap into everything with  one  interface instead of having to go into  10 or 12 different resources  to work up a variant  so in summary cancer genome  interpretation  and research require the right resources  and the landscape of genomic resources  is constantly  evolving and connecting with each other  it's a really exciting  time for genomic knowledge basis and  databases  and we can all benefit from the  collaborations that are being made  and i highly recommend if you have the  bandwidth or if you have the interest  to connect with groups like vic or  clindgen  or one and any of the knowledge based  groups  to um help further their efforts because  it's really it's really a neat  field to be in and then looking into the  future we can expect to see  a more connected landscape of resources  aggregation activity  and just overall comprehensiveness of  these resources  as they become more connected and then  we'll also  um look forward to seeing further  development  and incorporation of things like natural  language processing machine  learning artificial intelligence into  these resources as well  so i have a lot of people to acknowledge  thank you to the laboratories  committee for inviting me to come talk  today  thanks to the genomics of oncology  annotation team at mayo clinic  for supporting this work and really  driving these efforts  also thanks to the cancer genomics  consortium for  for their ongoing support and  collaboration as well and all of the  knowledge-based experts that have  contributed to my understanding and my  really nice experience with all of these  resources  so thanks so much for listening to me  today  um feel free to email me with any  questions find me on twitter or linkedin  as well  and have a great time during  this virtual event thanks so much
 

